346725} :answer-field/id #uuid "00632e9c-8030-5248-b276-8a6ab9ffed59" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347607}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "010c19af-739e-5fac-9299-70a1dfc77d8d" :question/math-academy-id "q-7813" :question/problem "What are the dimensions of the matrix $K$, where $K=\\begin{bmatrix}0\\end{bmatrix}?$" :question/worked-solution "The matrix $K$ has $1$ row  and $1$ column.  So,  its dimensions are  $1\\times 1.$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Dimensions of a Matrix with One Row or Column"}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088522 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088891 :tutorial/content "Some matrices have only one column, for instance\n\n$$\nA = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 13\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n$$\nB = \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n$$\nC = \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nHere, $A$ is a $2\\times 1$ matrix, $B$ is a $4\\times 1$ matrix, and $C$ is a $3\\times 1$ matrix. These types of matrices are often called **column vectors**.\n\nOther matrices have only one row, for instance\n\n$$\nD = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n$$\nE = \\begin{bmatrix}13 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n$$\nF = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nHere, $D$ is a $1\\times 2$ matrix, $E$ is a $1\\times 3$ matrix, and $F$ is a $1\\times 4$ matrix. These types of matrices are often called **row vectors**.\n\n**Note:** Every number can be thought of as a $1\\times 1$ matrix. For instance, we can consider the number $5$ as the matrix\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "1628e679-983a-5be7-9af6-81f0f45fa29a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2984 :tutorial/title "Matrices With One Row or Column"}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088534 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088898 :tutorial/content "A **square matrix** is a matrix where the number of rows is equal to the number of columns.\n\nFor example, the matrix\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis a square matrix because it has $2$ rows and $2$ columns. Its dimensions are $2 \\times 2.$\n\nIn general, the dimensions of a square matrix are $n \\times n.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "06286713-0de9-5c18-959f-04de81bee510" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3115 :tutorial/title "Square Matrices"}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088549 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087713 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087511 :question/id #uuid "1fcce090-8441-5547-ab52-18b0ff41ac5b" :question/math-academy-id "e-5017" :question/problem "Which of the following is a $3 \\times 2$ matrix?\n\n$$\nA = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 1.1 & \\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n$$\nB = \\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ 7 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n$$\nC = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "- The matrix $A$ has $2$ rows and $3$ columns. So, its dimensions are $2 \\times 3.$ $\\quad{\\color{red}\\times}$\n\n- The matrix $B$ has $3$ rows and $2$ columns. So, its dimensions are $3 \\times 2.$ $\\quad{\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n\n- The matrix $C$ has $1$ row and $3$ columns. So, its dimensions are $1 \\times 3.$ $\\quad{\\color{red}\\times}$\n\nTherefore, only $B$ is a $3 \\times 2$ matrix."} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ed7286fe-b6e2-5544-8bc6-86afc6cdb813" :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476102} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088275 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ee46ad41-124c-53b8-b6fe-63c0a88d4fe8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439889} {:answer/id #uuid "8629d8d0-9ca6-56ce-867f-945ea13c3797" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439890}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186439889} :answer-field/id #uuid "e29f9d5a-51a