 So, its dimensions are $2\\times 3.$ $\\,\\times $\n\n- The matrix $N$ has $3$ rows and $3$ columns. So, its dimensions are $3\\times 3.$ $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, only $N$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476096 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f35ed92-67dc-5e42-b374-d0c7e8405853" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476015} {:answer/id #uuid "bb419b41-076b-566f-ae1c-adbc9a7ffd06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476070}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476070} :answer-field/id #uuid "3f2e045a-dad7-5089-8346-f0284745230c" :answer-field/key "A" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476024} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "007b3975-65a8-5413-b36a-b9448f221cd6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476002} {:answer/id #uuid "8b080334-d984-5f31-a4fd-acc19f63cc8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476053}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476002} :answer-field/id #uuid "8548c722-2709-50df-a821-ba0277d85aab" :answer-field/key "C" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476052} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26023443-0660-5c5e-bbae-86d947803efc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476017} {:answer/id #uuid "26831adf-9ff0-53f0-bc31-e8bf1ad7ab85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476018}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476017} :answer-field/id #uuid "cc70ee49-90ab-56ac-a54f-3b376665a34b" :answer-field/key "B" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476078}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "f9fa0847-eb01-5dd2-be09-73c41b346b73" :question/problem "Which of the following is a $4\\times 3$ matrix?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n$$\nA = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 5 \\\\ -2 & -0.1 \\\\ -0.1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n$$\nB = \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 5 & 5 \\\\ 3 & -2 & -0.1 \\\\ -1 & 1.1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n$$\nC = \\begin{bmatrix}1.1 & \\sqrt{2} & -0.1 \\\\ -2 & -0.1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "- The matrix $A$ has $4$ rows and $2$ columns. So, its dimensions are $4\\times 2.$ $\\,\\times $\n\n- The matrix $B$ has $4$ rows and $3$ columns. So, its dimensions are $4\\times 3.$ $\\,✓$\n\n- The matrix $C$ has $3$ rows and $3$ columns. So, its dimensions are $3\\times 3.$ $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, only $B$ is a $4\\times 3$ matrix."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476097 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7dfbf4c9-b1c4-549c-80ea-2c3adecee66f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476048} {:answer/id #uuid "b9fa25ad-4b66-5943-a3c9-f1dedb2d52c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476069}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476069} :answer-field/id #uuid "0296401d-1721-5e67-a2b7-c58a10f0590f" :answer-field/key "G" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476003} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3ff896a2-a7d2-5bb9-92f7-a599db8404a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476025} {:answer/id #uuid "43edb404-7674-5761-ab18-45c830f87ac1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476028}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476028} :answer-field/id #uuid "42663f7a-38a4-5cc4-8f6a-efe7b6b220eb" :answer-field/key "E" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476027} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a27c788c-44c9-58c2-bd76-91c916f6370e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476063} {:answer/id #uuid "ac4b9116-661d-5172-abe3-c6f3a5913267" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476064}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476063} :answer-field/id #uuid "dee260e6-6241-5c72-aa2d-ad4949f179bc" :answer-field/key "F" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476089}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "fb51fb5a-4174-5854-a7d1-95ace128827c" :question/problem "Which of the following is a $1\\times 2$ matrix?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n$$\nE = \\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{3}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n$$\nF = \\begin{bmatrix}-0.1 \\\\ \\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n$$\nG = \\begin{bmatrix}1.1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "- The matrix $E$ has $1$ row and $1$ column. So, its dimensions are $1\\times 1.$ $\\,\\times $\n\n- The matrix $F$ has $2$ rows and $1$ column. So, its dimensions are $2\\times 1.$ $\\,\\times $\n\n- The matrix $G$ has $1$ row and $2$ columns. So, its dimensions are $1\\times 2.$ $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, only $G$ is a $1\\times 2$ matrix."}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Matrix by Its Description"}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088725 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088984 :tutorial/content "Matrices arise naturally in *many* real-world problems. Weather forecasting, image processing and computer graphics, video games, and online customization algorithms are just a few of the many applications of matrices. \n\nLet's consider an example where we can use a matrix to model bus routes in a city. \n\nThe diagram below shows the bus routes between three stations (Station A, Station B, and Station C) in a particular city, where $A, B,$ and $C$ represent the stations and the arrows represent the directed routes between them. \n\n![](images/cb/cbdf5c2470229ae3ba10ee18bccd2dc764407897397466b157474bf2b819f5d9.png)\n\nNote that the circles $A,B,$ and $C$ in our diagram are called **nodes**.\n\nWe wish to represent this same diagram using a matrix. Let's start by putting this information into a table like the one below:\n\n| From / To | **A** | **B** | **C** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| **A** |  |  |  |\n| **B** |  |  |  |\n| **C** |  |  |  |\n\nLet's now fill in the rows of our table. We begin by considering all of the bus routes from Station A to the other stations:\n\n- There are $\\color{blue}0$ bus routes from $A$ to itself.\n\n- There is $\\color{blue}1$ bus route from $A$ to $B.$\n\n- There is $\\color{blue}1$ bus route from $A$ to $C.$\n\nFilling in the first row of the table, we get the following:\n\n| From / To | **A** | **B** | **C** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| **A** | $\\color{blue} 0$ | $\\color{blue} 1$ | $\\color{blue} 1$ |\n| **B** |  |  |  |\n| **C** |  |  |  |\n\nFilling in the remaining rows in the same way, we get:\n\n| From / To | **A** | **B** | **C** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| **A** | $0$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n| **B** | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| **C** | $1$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n\nFinally, keeping only the essential information, we obtain the following matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "523d25fd-6b49-5cd7-ac05-80ac6fa0f3d8" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6114 :tutorial/title "Using Matrices for Modeling"}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088726 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087819 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087654 :question/id #uuid "5c3b6d53-3dbb-59f6-8d15-a9e6f48927a1" :question/math-academy-id "e-9942" :question/problem "![](images/9a/9af4801abdec165de2b720a21dd6a727c578e32cbae9c9c54bd589a1b65043b0.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows train routes between three train stations, where the nodes represent stations and arrows represent the directed routes between them. The (incomplete) route matrix below represents the number of routes between the three stations. Find the value of $a+b+c.$\n\n| From / To | **A** | **B** | **C** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| **A** | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}a}$ | $1$ | $0$ |\n| **B** | $0$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}b}$ | $2$ |\n| **C** | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}c}$ | $0$ | $1$ |" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine our diagram.\n\n- There is $1$ arrow starting at Station A and ending at Station A. So, there is only one route from $A$ to $A,$ and so $a=1.$\n\n- No arrows are starting at Station B and ending at Station B. So, there are no routes from $B$ to $B,$ and so $b=0.$\n\n- There is $1$ arrow starting at Station C and ending at Station A. So, there is only one route from $C$ to $A,$ and so $c=1.$\n\nAs a result, our route matrix is the following:\n\n| From / To | **A** | **B** | **C** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| **A** | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}1}$ | $1$ | $0$ |\n| **B** | $0$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0}$ | $2$ |\n| **C** | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}1}$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\na+b+c = 1+0+1 = 2.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "10ca3efc-7d23-5bc1-ab41-f785bed69986" :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476174} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088004 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8c96a891-effe-5393-9122-2fd45c7880a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084759} {:answer/id #uuid "a56af5ae-6c68-5eec-9691-9f0c10aa2a51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084760} {:answer/id #uuid "d3dbeccf-f8e6-5f67-94e6-b71395980682" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084761} {:answer/id #uuid "4b43ca89-d08b-5f23-9441-eb4c7490a968" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084762} {:answer/id #uuid "617b3af9-2e69-5480-bc94-4e56b348eb5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084763}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084761} :answer-field/id #uuid "1ce292f8-0fb7-5fa5-a53d-17222ef53621" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084758}] :question/id #uuid "aa9a6edf-7f79-5b79-bc3e-d65f844a9280" :question/math-academy-id "q-138940" :question/problem "![](images/cb/cbdf5c2470229ae3ba10ee18bccd2dc764407897397466b157474bf2b819f5d9.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows bus routes between three stations, where nodes represent the stations and arrows represent the directed routes between them. The (incomplete) route matrix below represents information about the number of routes between each of the three stations. Find the value of $a + b + c$.\n\n| From / To | **A** | **B** | **C** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| **A** | $0$ | $a$ | $1$ |\n| **B** | $0$ | $b$ | $c$ |\n| **C** | $1$ | $0$ | $0$ |" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine our diagram.\n\n- There is $1$ arrow starting at Station A and ending at Station B. So, there is only one route from $A$ to $B$, and so $a=1$.\n\n- There is $1$ arrow starting at Station B and ending at Station B. So, there is only one route from $B$ to $B$, and so $b=1$.\n\n- There are $2$ arrows starting at Station B and ending at Station C. So, there are $2$ routes from $B$ to $C$, and so $c=2$.\n\nAs a result, our route matrix is the following:\n\n| From / To | **A** | **B** | **C** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| **A** | $0$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n| **B** | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| **C** | $1$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\na+b+c = 1+1+2 = 4.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088005 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f69d692d-3974-51b1-bb04-18be51794029" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084765} {:answer/id #uuid "fc29d722-413d-5894-bf5f-95abc1c9856f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084766} {:answer/id #uuid "0af225f0-740d-5c01-94b8-d23cc84474e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084767} {:answer/id #uuid "50d64707-745b-5042-b82b-fced40ba99cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084768} {:answer/id #uuid "74cb046b-f4a1-557c-92a2-b83dcc3303a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084769}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084768} :answer-field/id #uuid "8bdfb307-7e4e-5a8c-b404-848dcc68751e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084764}] :question/id #uuid "725a18d5-fc0f-5026-9a9b-e7e5e2784b06" :question/math-academy-id "q-139685" :question/problem "![](images/ab/abbc0e5ce20a52263ba067d1563708f803e33def4f931a3d3badedd1c8ff4908.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows train routes between three stations, where nodes represent the stations and arrows represent the directed lines between them. The (incomplete) route matrix below represents information about the number of routes between each of the three stations. Find the value of $a + b + c$.\n\n| From / To | **A** | **B** | **C** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| **A** | $a$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n| **B** | $b$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n| **C** | $1$ | $c$ | $0$ |" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine our diagram.\n\n- There are no arrows starting at Station A and ending at Station A. So, there are no routes from $A$ to $A$, and so $a=0$.\n\n- There are no arrows starting at Station B and ending at Station A. So, there are no routes from $B$ to $A$, and so $b=0$.\n\n- There is $1$ arrow starting at Station C and ending at Station B. So, there is only one route from $C$ to $B$, and so $c=1$.\n\nAs a result, our route matrix is the following:\n\n| From / To | **A** | **B** | **C** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| **A** | $0$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n| **B** | $0$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n| **C** | $1$ | $1$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\na+b+c = 0+0+1 = 1.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186271964 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "257b433d-960b-5602-9139-f0a08f725b10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269602} {:answer/id #uuid "2912ecc2-b82b-545b-a134-0f849b9f6f10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269638} {:answer/id #uuid "a28f67e7-b07f-5e99-99f1-6ef0ca27df71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270719} {:answer/id #uuid "ab496260-2eb5-5f24-b36a-b5c83e218fef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270799} {:answer/id #uuid "cee52816-3d9a-5a2d-bf35-0841387ab28c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271084}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269638} :answer-field/id #uuid "2a50b6d4-0ed8-59a9-bfe9-13d431937f58" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271588}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "cf86f867-a1a5-56dc-89e8-e2255e71399b" :question/math-academy-id "q-139689" :question/problem "![](images/6c/6c52f315d079b82fb729c4ab47f210ec4bf2683d0dfe1adfe79c94db9fbc5f63.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows train routes between three stations, where nodes represent the stations and arrows represent the directed routes between them. The (incomplete) route matrix below represents information about the number of routes between each of the three stations. Find the value of $a+b+c.$\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"10\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td align=\"center\" colspan=\"3\">To:</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\"><b>A</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\"><b>B</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\"><b>C</b></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"3\">From:</td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\"><b>A</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$a$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\"><b>B</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$b$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\"><b>C</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$c$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine our diagram.\n\n- There is $1$ arrow starting at Station A and ending at Station B. So, there is only one route from $A$ to $B,$ and so $a=1.$\n\n- There is $1$ arrow starting at Station B and ending at Station B. So, there is only one route from $B$ to $B,$ and so $b=1.$\n\n- There are no arrows starting at Station C and ending at Station A. So, there are no routes from $C$ to $A,$ and so $c=0.$\n\nAs a result, our route matrix is the following:\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"10\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td align=\"center\" colspan=\"3\">To:</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\"><b>A</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\"><b>B</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\"><b>C</b></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"3\">From:</td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\"><b>A</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\"><b>B</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\"><b>C</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\na+b+c=1+1+0=2.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186271965 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "53144731-7725-5907-918b-d5713b670e0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270007} {:answer/id #uuid "5a44cfba-69bd-514f-beb9-1f9c988affe3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270072} {:answer/id #uuid "60c809fd-5f78-5ac9-aa54-4cdd1b54286e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270124} {:answer/id #uuid "70398bab-0b9a-5aa7-adad-d0d070ded60a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270262} {:answer/id #uuid "e913dca1-03c6-5d39-8cb8-3922ed7d4b59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271307}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186271307} :answer-field/id #uuid "35c51080-e239-5221-93b7-5dbdc5f93e34" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271614}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "6e096d82-d20e-57bf-96a5-ec2c24ce0473" :question/math-academy-id "q-139719" :question/problem "![](images/0e/0ed581569bdc7c4f269f4cdf48d3ae20205627312641ff5b06daa2dca5a0e168.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows bus routes between four stations, where nodes represent the stations and arrows represent the directed routes between them. The (incomplete) route matrix below represents information about the number of routes between each of the four stations. Find the value of $a+b+c.$\n\n| From / To | **A** | **B** | **C** | **D** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| **A** | $0$ | $1$ | $0$ | $a$ |\n| **B** | $0$ | $0$ | $b$ | $0$ |\n| **C** | $0$ | $0$ | $c$ | $1$ |\n| **D** | $0$ | $0$ | $1$ | $0$ |" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine our diagram.\n\n- There are $2$ arrows starting at Station A and ending at Station D. So, there are two routes from $A$ to $D,$ and so $a=2.$\n\n- There is $1$ arrow starting at Station B and ending at Station C. So, there is only one route from $B$ to $C,$ and so $b=1.$\n\n- There are no arrows starting at Station C and ending at Station C. So, there are no routes from $C$ to $C,$ and so $c=0.$\n\nAs a result, our route matrix is the following:\n\n| From / To | **A** | **B** | **C** | **D** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| **A** | $0$ | $1$ | $0$ | $2$ |\n| **B** | $0$ | $0$ | $1$ | $0$ |\n| **C** | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n| **D** | $0$ | $0$ | $1$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\na+b+c=2+1+0=3.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299744 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ffc48ff-1011-56e8-978d-23ac82a79306" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297690}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297690} :answer-field/id #uuid "28ebc2c3-8f30-59dc-a757-e2f3006a8b32" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298775} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8ab4fcc6-76c4-5164-a6f8-e99687699fd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298155}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186298155} :answer-field/id #uuid "9e857817-9ed7-5435-956e-848cd74bf5b0" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299159} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dfcfbf74-a02e-5cd5-884b-9d1f0073aa1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :